+## Password reset
+
+A user who has forgotten their password cannot use `passwd`, which needs the
+current one. Instead an administrator issues a one-time reset token and passes
+it on to the user:
+
+```
+python -m accounts --store PATH reset-token USERNAME [--ttl SECONDS]
+```
+
+`reset-token` prints the token on a line of its own on standard output, and
+nothing else, so it can be captured directly (`token=$(… reset-token alice)`).
+The token is valid for `--ttl` seconds, 900 (15 minutes) by default; `--ttl`
+must be a positive whole number of seconds and is accepted only by this
+command.
+
+`reset USERNAME` then reads the token and the new password from standard
+input, one per line. With a valid token it sets the new password and prints
+`password reset`. A wrong, already used or expired token prints
+`invalid token` on standard error and exits with status 1; a new password
+shorter than 12 characters exits with status 2 and leaves the token usable for
+another attempt. Either command fails with status 3 for a username that does
+not exist.
+
+A token works only once, only for the account it was issued for, and only
+until it expires. Issuing a token replaces any token the account still had, so
+only the most recent one works. Neither command needs the old password, and
+both work on a locked account — a successful `reset` also clears the lock and
+the failed-attempt counter, so the user can log in again straight away.
+
